#!/bin/bash
# ============================================================== #
version=rescript-v3.0
usage="
Author		: Sulfuror, Copyright (c) 2018 <sulfuror@gmail.com>
URL		: https://gitlab.com/sulfuror/rescript.sh
License		: BSD 2-clause 'Simplified' License
Version		: $version
Description	: rescript is a bash script created to manage
		  backups made with restic program.

'restic is a backup program which allows saving multiple revisions 
of files and directories in an encrypted repository 
stored on different backends.'

For more information about restic visit https://restic.net

This script will run backup, snapshots, forget, prune, check and
stats commands automatically by just indicating the name given
to your repository. e.g.:

  rescript [repo_name]

That said, all restic commands are available within this script
except for [backup], which exists but it will use your configuration
file to perform a backup.

Usage:
  rescript [repo_name] [commands] [--flags]
  
Rescript commands:
  backup		Take a snapshot using the values set in
  			your configuration file.
  cleanup		Forget and prune according to the policies
  			in your configuration file.
  config		Rescript configuration; set your default
  			text editor, open, delete or modify your
  			configuration and exclusion files.
  editor		Change the default rescript text editor used
  			to open your configuration and exclusions files.
  help			Display rescript help and usage.
  install		Install rescript (this just move the script
  			to your .local/bin directory for convenience.
  logs			This command needs a flag to view, list
			or delete your log files.
  mounter		Mount a restic repo.
  restorer		Restore a restic snapshot.			
  unlocker		Remove lock created by rescript.
  version		Display rescript version.

Rescript flags:
  --help		This flag is available for all rescript commands
  			and it can be used to display the usage for every
  			single rescript command.
  --log			This flag is available only for [backup], [cleanup],
  			[restorer] and [check] when using [--random] flag.
  			If used, it must be after all commands, flags and
  			options; this will create a log file with the
  			command output.
  --random		This flag is available for [check] command only;
  			it will execute check using [--read-data-subset]
  			selecting a random number between 1-10 out of 10
  			groups.
"
# ============================================================== #
